WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:04.640
Film Courage: Kathie, what is then difference
between a low and high-concept movie?

00:00:04.640 --> 00:00:06.609
And which one favors new writers?

00:00:06.609 --> 00:00:13.250
Kathie Fong Yoneda: I think that the low-concept
favors new writers only because I think studios

00:00:13.250 --> 00:00:19.789
in general are more willing to take a chance
on something that maybe is lower in budget.

00:00:19.789 --> 00:00:25.369
But concept and budget are two different things.

00:00:25.369 --> 00:00:32.340
A high-concept movie usually means a higher-concept
budget in general and that would be like your

00:00:32.340 --> 00:00:37.930
action pictures, your super-hero pictures,
things like STAR WARS.

00:00:37.930 --> 00:00:42.390
Things that have what they call the “tentpole”
(you’ve probably heard that term before)

00:00:42.390 --> 00:00:50.120
a tentpole effect…it’s just a lot different.

00:00:50.120 --> 00:00:54.210
And most of them you’ll notice are based
on something that has been adapted from another

00:00:54.210 --> 00:01:01.380
piece of material like comics, a book, novels,
that kind of thing.

00:01:01.380 --> 00:01:09.560
But for new people (new writers coming in),
writing a nice project that is in another

00:01:09.560 --> 00:01:14.200
arena that doesn’t require a huge budget
and a lot of special effects, that’s a way

00:01:14.200 --> 00:01:18.499
to really get a sense of can they develop
characters?

00:01:18.499 --> 00:01:23.399
Can they carry a story all the way through
for 90 minutes to 100 minutes?

00:01:23.399 --> 00:01:28.850
And can they give us good roles that actors
want?

00:01:28.850 --> 00:01:35.639
So I think that is better, if you try and
do a lower-budget, lower-concept in terms

00:01:35.639 --> 00:01:37.060
of genre.

00:01:37.060 --> 00:01:39.110
That’s the way to do it.

00:01:39.110 --> 00:01:42.929
Film Courage: Just a reminder, what is the
definition of high-concept and low-concept

00:01:42.929 --> 00:01:43.929
again?

00:01:43.929 --> 00:01:51.369
Kathie Fong Yoneda: Well high-concept usually
means something that you can do a lot more

00:01:51.369 --> 00:01:55.729
with it in terms of whether it can be sequels
or prequels.

00:01:55.729 --> 00:02:03.939
Those tend to be more high-budget and more
high-concept in terms of it’s a larger world,

00:02:03.939 --> 00:02:09.360
more characters and they can do (like I said)
more with it.

00:02:09.360 --> 00:02:14.290
They can continue on an adventure with all
of those characters.

00:02:14.290 --> 00:02:19.510
So that’s why a lot of them you’ll see
are adapted from other pieces of material.

00:02:19.510 --> 00:02:25.170
Film Courage: What’s a good test if someone
thinks their script is high-concept?

00:02:25.170 --> 00:02:30.220
How can they run it through some tests because
maybe they’ll find out it’s actually low-concept

00:02:30.220 --> 00:02:31.750
and they’re just not aware?

00:02:31.750 --> 00:02:36.459
Kathie Fong Yoneda: I think genre is one way.

00:02:36.459 --> 00:02:40.730
If you look at the ones that usually make
the most money or that are based on other

00:02:40.730 --> 00:02:49.040
pieces of material, a lot of times they fall
into science-fiction, horror, fantasy or sci-fi

00:02:49.040 --> 00:02:50.040
fantasy.

00:02:50.040 --> 00:02:52.870
Those are the most popular genres for that.

00:02:52.870 --> 00:02:58.150
Certainly if you look at things that are action
adventure like THE BOURNE IDENTITY, that’s

00:02:58.150 --> 00:03:04.040
another one that you can do a lot with because
there is always going to be a new adventure

00:03:04.040 --> 00:03:05.950
as this guy [Jason Bourne] is trying to find
out his real identity.

00:03:05.950 --> 00:03:06.620
Question for the Viewers: Should new writers
focus on high-concept or low-concept?

